"A conjecture on partitions of groups (https://arxiv.org/abs/1408.6259)\nciteturn4academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 12.2 years ago; Authors: Igor Protasov, Sergii Slobodianiuk ... We conjecture that every infinite group $G$ can be partitioned into countably many cells $G=\\bigcup_{n\\in\\omega}A_n$ such that $cov(A_nA_n^{-1})=|G|$ for each $n\\in\\omega$.\nTitle: A conjecture on partitions of groups\nAuthors: Igor Protasov, Sergii Slobodianiuk\nDate: Tue Aug 26 21:06:42 2014\n\nWe conjecture that every infinite group $G$ can be partitioned into countably many cells $G=\\bigcup_{n\\in\\omega}A_n$ such that $cov(A_nA_n^{-1})=|G|$ for each $n\\in\\omega$. Here $cov(A)=\\min\\{|X|:X\\subseteq G, G=XA\\}$. We confirm this conjecture for each group of regular cardinality and for some groups (in particular, Abelian) of an arbitrary cardinality.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA note on partitions of groups (https://arxiv.org/abs/1408.5607)\nciteturn4academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 12.2 years ago; Authors: Igor Protasov, Sergii Slobodianiuk ... Every infinite group $G$ of regular cardinality can be partitioned $G=A_1\\cup A_2$ so that $G\\neq FA_1$, $G\\neq FA_2$ for every subset $F\\subset G$ of cardinality $|F|<|G|$.\nTitle: A note on partitions of groups\nAuthors: Igor Protasov, Sergii Slobodianiuk\nDate: Sun Aug 24 13:57:27 2014\n\nEvery infinite group $G$ of regular cardinality can be partitioned $G=A_1\\cup A_2$ so that $G\\neq FA_1$, $G\\neq FA_2$ for every subset $F\\subset G$ of cardinality $|F|<|G|$. The first author asked whether the same is true for each group $G$ of singular cardinality. We show that an answer depends on the algebraic structure of $G$. In particular, this is so for each free group but the statement does not hold for every Abelian group $G$ of singular cardinality. As an application, we prove that every Abelian group of singular cardinality k admits maximal translation invariant k-bounded topology that impossible for all groups of regular cardinality.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA CONJECTURE ON PARTITIONS OF GROUPS (https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=98bc4f3377d2493e85b02a8edc66764a29621607&repid=rep1&type=pdf)\nciteturn4search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; A CONJECTURE ON PARTITIONS OF GROUPS\nIGOR PROTASOV, SERGII SLOBODIANIUK ... For any ﬁnite partition of an inﬁnite group, at last one cell of the partition has a rich\nA CONJECTURE ON PARTITIONS OF GROUPS\nIGOR PROTASOV, SERGII SLOBODIANIUK\nAbstract. We conjecture that every inﬁnite group G can be partitioned into countably many\ncells G = S\nn∈ω\nAn such that cov(AnA−1\nn ) = |G| for each n ∈ω. Here cov(A) = min{|X| : X ⊆\nG, G = XA}. We conﬁrm this conjecture for each group of regular cardinality and for some\ngroups (in particular, Abelian) of an arbitrary cardinality.\nIntroduction\nFor any ﬁnite partition of an inﬁnite group, at last one cell of the partition has a rich\ncombinatorial structure. The Ramsey Theory of groups gives a plenty of concrete examples\n(see [4], [7])\nOn the other hand, the subsets of a group could be classiﬁed by their size. For corresponding\npartition problems see the survey [8].\nGiven a group G and a subset A of G, we denote\ncov(A) = min{|X| : X ⊆G, G = XA}.\nThe covering number cov(A) evaluates a size of A inside G and, if A is a subgroup, coincides\nwith the index |G : A|.\nIt is easy to partition each inﬁnite group G = A1∪A2 so that cov(A1) and cov(A2) are inﬁnite.\nMoreover, if |G| is regular, there is a partition G =\nS\nα<|G|\nHα such that cov(G\\Hα) = |G| for each\narXiv:1408.6259v1  [math.GR]  26 Aug 2014\nα < |G|. In particular, there is a partition G = A1 ∪A2 such that cov(A1) = cov(A2) = |G|.\nSee [5], [9], [10] for these statements, their generalizations and applications.\nHowever, for every n ∈N, there is a (minimal) natural number Φ(n) such that, for every\ngroup G and every partition G = A1 ∪... ∪An, cov(AiA−1\ni ) ≤Φ(n) for some cell Ai of the\npartition. It is still an open problem posed in [6, Problem 13.44] whether Φ(n) = n. For the\nhistory and results behind this problem see the survey [1].\nIn [2, Question F], J. Erde asked whether, given a partition P of an inﬁnite group G such\nthat |P| < |G|, there is A ∈P such that cov(AA−1) is ﬁnite. After some simple examples\nanswering this question extremely negatively, we run into the following conjecture.\nConjecture. Every inﬁnite group G of cardinality κ can be partitioned G = S\nn<ω\nAn so that\ncov(AnA−1\nn ) = κ for each n ∈ω.\nIn this note we conﬁrm Conjecture for every group of regular cardinality and for some groups\n(in particular, Abelian) of an arbitrary cardinality.\n2010 Mathematics Subject Classiﬁcation. 03E05, 20B07, 20F69.\nKey words and phrases. partition of groups, covering number.\n1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Note: A conjecture on partitions of groups (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/265128141_Note_A_conjecture_on_partitions_of_groups)\nciteturn4search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 months ago; # Note: A conjecture on partitions of groups ... IGOR PROTASOV, SERGII SLOBODIANIUK ... For any ﬁnite partition of an inﬁnite group, at last one cell of the partition has a rich\nArticle PDF Available\n\n# Note: A conjecture on partitions of groups\n\n  * August 2014\n  * Combinatorica 36(3)\n\nDOI:10.1007/s00493-014-3208-y\n\nAuthors:\n\nIgor Vladimirovich Protasov\n\n  * Taras Shevchenko National University of Kyiv\n\nSergii Slobodianiuk\n\n  * Kyiv School of Economics\n\nImage\n\nDownload full-text PDFRead full-text\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe conjecture that every infinite group G can be partitioned into countably many cells $G=\\bigcup_{n\\in\\omega}A_n$ such that $cov(A_nA_n^{-1})=|G|$ for each $n\\in\\omega$. Here $cov(A)=\\min\\{|X|:X\\subseteq G, G=XA\\}$. We confirm this conjecture for each group of regular cardinality and for some groups (in particular, Abelian) of an arbitrary cardinality.\n\n\nPublic Full-text 1\n\nContent uploaded by Igor Vladimirovich Protasov\n\nAuthor content\n\nAll content in this area was uploaded by Igor Vladimirovich Protasov on Sep 08, 2014\n\nContent may be subject to copyright.\n\narXiv:1408.6259v1 [math.GR] 26 Aug 2014\n\nA CONJECTURE ON PARTITIONS OF GROUPS\n\nIGOR PROTASOV, SERGII SLOBODIANIUK\n\nAbstract. We conjecture that every inﬁnite group G can be partitioned into countably many\n\ncells G=S\n\nn∈ω\n\nA n such that cov(A n A−1\n\nn) = |G|for each n∈ω. Here cov(A) = min{|X|:X⊆\n\nG, G =XA}. We conﬁrm this conjecture for each group of regular cardinality and for some\n\ngroups (in particular, Abelian) of an arbitrary cardinality.\n\nIntroduction\n\nFor any ﬁnite partition of an inﬁnite group, at last one cell of the partition has a rich\n\ncombinatorial structure. The Ramsey Theory of groups gives a plenty of concrete examples\n\n(see [4], [7])\n\nKey words and phrases. partition of groups, covering number.\n\n1\n\n2 IGOR PROTASOV, SERGII SLOBODIANIUK\n\nResults\n\nFor a cardinal κ, we denote by cf(κ) the coﬁnality of κ.\n\nTheorem 1. Let G be an inﬁnite group of cardinality κ. Then there exists a partition G=\n\nS\n\nn∈ω\n\nA n such that cov(A n A−1\n\nn)≥cf(κ)for each n∈ω.\n\nProof. If G is countable, the statement is trivial: take any partition of G into ﬁnite subsets.\n\nFor κ>ℵ 0, we choose a family {G α:α < κ}of subgroups of G such that\n\n(1) G 0={e}and G=S\n\nα<κ\n\nG α,e is the identity of G;\n\n(2) G α⊂G β for all α < β < κ;\n\n(3) S\n\nα<β\n\nG α=G β for each limit ordinal β < κ;\n\n(4) |G α|<κ for each α < κ.\n\nFollowing [11], for each α < κ, we decompose G α+1 \\G α into right cosets by G α and choose\n\nsome system X α of representatives so G α+1 =G α X α. Take an arbitrary element g∈G\\ {e}\n\nand choose the smallest subgroup G α with g∈G α. By (3), α=α 1+ 1 for some ordinal α 1<κ.\n\nHence g∈G α 1+1 \\G α 1 and there exists g 1∈G α 1,x α 1∈X α 1 such that g=g 1 x α 1. If g 1 6=e,\n\nwe choose the ordinal α 2 and elements g 2∈G α 2+1 \\G α 2 and x α 2∈X α 2 such that g 1=g 2 x α 2.\n\nSince the set of ordinals {α:α < κ}is well-ordered, after ﬁnite number s(g) of steps, we get\n\nthe representation\n\ng=x α s(g)x α s(g)−1...x α 2 x α 1,x α i∈X α i.\n\nWe note that this representation is unique and put\n\nRemark 1.\n\nIt is well-known [3, Theorems 23.1 and 24.1] that each Abelian group can be embedded\n\ninto the direct product of countable groups. Applying Theorem 2, we conﬁrm conjecture for\n\nAbelian groups. Moreover, if a group G of cardinality κ has an Abelian homomorphic image\n\nof cardinality κ(in particular, if G is a free group of rank κ) then Conjecture is valid for G.\n\nRemark 2.\n\nEvery inﬁnite group G can be written as a union G=S\n\nα<cf κ\n\nH α of subsets of cardinality <κ\n\n(and so cov(H α H−1\n\nα) = κ). Hence, Conjecture holds also if cf κ=ℵ 0.\n\nReferences\n\n[1] T. Banakh, I. Protasov, S. Slobodianiuk Densities, submeasures and partitions of groups, preprint\n\n(http://arxiv.org/abs/1303:4612).\n\n[2] J. Erde, A note on combinatorial derivation, preprint (http://arxiv.org/abs/1210:7622).\n\n[3] L. Fucks, Inﬁnite Abelian Group, vol. 1, Academic Press, 1970.\n\n[4] N. Hindman, D. Strauss, Algebra in the Stone- ˇ\n\nC ech compactiﬁcation, De Gruyter, 2nd edition, 2012.\n\n[5] V.I. Malykhin, I.V. Protasov, Maximal resolvability of bounded groups, Topology Appl. 20 (1996), 1–6.\n\n[6] V.D. Mazurov, E.I. Khukhro Unsolved problems in group theory, the Kaurovka notebook, 13-th augmented\n\nedition, Novosibirsk, 1995.\n\n[7] I.V. Protasov, Combinatorics of numbers, VNTL Publ. Lviv, 1997.\n\n[8] I. Protasov, Selective survey on Subset Combinatorics of Groups, Ukr. Math. Bull. 7(2010), 220-257.\n\n[9] I.V. Protasov, Resolvability of τ-bounded groups, Matem. Stud. 5(1995), 17–20.\n\n[10] I. Protasov, Cellularity and density of balleans, Appl. General Topology 8(2007), 283–291.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPartitions of groups into large subsets (https://arxiv.org/abs/1408.5606)\nciteturn4academia15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 12.2 years ago; Authors: Igor Protasov, Sergii Slobodianiuk ... It is known that every infinite group G can be partitioned into countably many \\aleph_0-large subsets.\nTitle: Partitions of groups into large subsets\nAuthors: Igor Protasov, Sergii Slobodianiuk\nDate: Sun Aug 24 13:45:31 2014\n\nLet G be a group and let k be a cardinal. A subset A of G is called left (right) k-large if there exists a subset F of G such that |F| < { and G = FA (G = AF). We say that A is k-large if A is left and right k-large. It is known that every infinite group G can be partitioned into countably many \\aleph_0-large subsets. On the other hand, every amenable (in particular Abelian) group G cannot be partitioned into > \\aleph_0 \\aleph_0-large subsets. We prove that every infinite group G of cardinality k can be partitioned into k left- \\aleph_1-large subsets and every free group F_k in the infinite alphabet k can be partitioned into k 4-large subsets.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA NOTE ON PARTITIONS OF GROUPS (https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=4b7c42f05cb3ac9aa879dfd401a2bf25aca2a469&repid=rep1&type=pdf)\nciteturn4search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10 months ago; IGOR PROTASOV, SERGII SLOBODIANIUK ... Every inﬁnite group G of regular cardinality can be partitioned G = A1 ∪A2 so that ... Key words and phrases. partitions of groups, large and thick subsets of a group, κ-bounded topology, maximal\nA NOTE ON PARTITIONS OF GROUPS\nIGOR PROTASOV, SERGII SLOBODIANIUK\nAbstract. Every inﬁnite group G of regular cardinality can be partitioned G = A1 ∪A2 so that\nG ̸= FA1, G ̸= FA2 for every subset F ⊂G of cardinality |F| < |G|. In [3, Problem 13.45], the\nﬁrst author asked whether the same is true for each group G of singular cardinality. We show that\nan answer depends on the algebraic structure of G. In particular, this is so for each free group but\nthe statement does not hold for every Abelian group G of singular cardinality. As an application,\nwe prove that every Abelian group of singular cardinality κ admits maximal translation invariant\nκ-bounded topology that impossible for all groups of regular cardinality.\n1. Introduction\nLet G be a group and let κ be a cardinal, κ ≤|G|. We denote by [G]<κ the family of all subsets\nof G of cardinality < κ, and by cfκ the coﬁnality of κ, so an inﬁnite cardinal κ is regular if and\nonly if cfκ = κ. We say that a subset A of G is\n• left (right) κ-thick if, for every F ∈[G]<κ there exists a ∈A such that Fa ⊆A (aF ⊆A);\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nМатематичнi Студiї. Т.42, №2 (https://irbis-nbuv.gov.ua/cgi-bin/irbis_nbuv/cgiirbis_64.exe?C21COM=2&I21DBN=UJRN&IMAGE_FILE_DOWNLOAD=1&Image_file_name=PDF%2FMat_st_2014_42_2_3.pdf&P21DBN=UJRN)\nciteturn4search17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11.7 years ago; Protasov, S. Slobodianiuk ... In Sections 8, 9 we partition a group into thick and thin subsets and conclude the paper ... Keywords: Partitions of groups; large, thick, small, thin subsets of a group; ﬁltration; submeasure.\nМатематичнi Студiї. Т.42, №2\nMatematychni Studii. V.42, No.2\nУДК 512.5\nI. Protasov, S. Slobodianiuk\nPARTITIONS OF GROUPS\nI. Protasov, S. Slobodianiuk. Partitions of groups, Mat. Stud. 42 (2014), 115–128.\nWe classify the subsets of a group by their sizes, formalize the basic methods of partitions\nand apply them to partition a group to subsets of prescribed sizes.\nI. Протасов, С. Слободянюк. Разбиения групп // Мат. Студiї. – 2014. – Т.42, №2. – C.115–\n128.\nКлассифицированы подмножества групп по их размерам, формализированы основные\nметоды разбиений и применины к разбиениям групп на подмножества предписанных раз-\nмеров.\n1. Introduction. In Partition Combinatorics, one of the main areas is Ramsey Theory. The\nmotto of this theory could be “An absolute chaos is impossible”: if a set X is endowed with\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSyndetic submeasures and partitions of $G$-spaces and groups (https://arxiv.org/abs/1210.5804)\nciteturn4academia18 [wordlim: 200] Published: 14.0 years ago; Authors: Taras Banakh, Igor Protasov, Sergiy Slobodianiuk ... We prove that for every number k each countable infinite group $G$ admits a partition $G=A\\cup B$ into two sets which are $k$-meager in the sense that for every $k$-element subset $K\\subset G$ the sets $KA$ and $KB$ are not thick.\nTitle: Syndetic submeasures and partitions of $G$-spaces and groups\nAuthors: Taras Banakh, Igor Protasov, Sergiy Slobodianiuk\nDate: Mon Oct 22 04:58:58 2012\n\nWe prove that for every number k each countable infinite group $G$ admits a partition $G=A\\cup B$ into two sets which are $k$-meager in the sense that for every $k$-element subset $K\\subset G$ the sets $KA$ and $KB$ are not thick. The proof is based on the fact that $G$ possesses a syndetic submeasure, i.e., a left-invariant submeasure $\\mu:\\mathcal P(G)\\to[0,1]$ such that for each $\\epsilon > 1/|G|$ and subset $A\\subset G$ with $\\mu(A)<1$ there is a set $B\\subset G\\setminus A$ such that $\\mu(B)<\\epsilon$ and $FB=G$ for some finite subset $F\\subset G$.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) On invariant partitions of groups (https://www.academia.edu/171237119/On_invariant_partitions_of_groups)\nciteturn4search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago;  Key words and phrases. partition of a group; density; submeasure; amenable group. ... The first non-trivial n−1 result proved by this approach was the upper bound ΦX (n) ≤ 22 −1 proved in Theorem 12.7 of [19] for groups G endowed with the smallest ideal I = {∅} and generalized later by Slobodianiuk (see [17, 4